  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 421 AM MST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nThe advertised trough is now directly over the area, with the \nassociated cold front now moving east of the Divide. Precipitation \nwill continue early this morning, mostly east of the Divide, \nwith the Bighorns most favored from here on out. A Winter \nWeather Advisory is now in effect there given snow totals in the\n5 to 10 inch range and strong wind; winter travel conditions \nare ongoing across the highway passes. Snow levels will drop to \nthe basin floors, though right as precipitation starts to \ndecrease. Most areas will remain dry given the increasingly \nlimited coverage as the morning goes on, though portions of the \neastern Bighorn Basin and western Johnson County could see a \nlight dusting of snow by sunrise. \n\nMeanwhile, strong wind is ongoing across the wind corridor, most \nnotably across Natrona County. The Casper area, in particular, has \nand will continue to see the strongest gusts, currently observed in \nthe 70 to 90 mph range. A deep surface low across far northeastern \nWyoming and quickly increasing surface pressure to our southwest \nwill keep a tight gradient going here through the morning. Gusts \nwill start to come down by late morning as the gradient \nsubsides, though 35 to 45 mph gusts will persist into the \nevening.\n\nA cooler airmass will be in place behind this system, finally \nbringing an end to the widespread abnormally warm temperatures. \nAfter a brief transitory ridge Wednesday night, flow becomes \nsouthwesterly again on Thursday ahead of the next system. A leading \nshortwave ahead of the main trough will return precipitation to \nwestern Wyoming starting Thursday morning. This will be followed by \nthe arrival of the main trough on Friday, with precipitation chances \nbecoming more widespread through Friday night. However, this system \ndoes not look very impressive, with the trough becoming fairly \ndisorganized as it passes over Wyoming. The western mountains will \nagain be most favored, with only minimal amounts across the basins \nelsewhere. Regardless, colder temperatures will allow for most of \nthis to fall as snow, even if only a dusting across lower elevations.\n\nCooler and unsettled conditions are expected to continue on Saturday \nas another system slides by to our north. Precipitation with this \none looks minimal, with the best chances across northern Wyoming. \nFurther ahead, ensemble guidance favors the active pattern to \ncontinue through early next week with several more chances for \nprecipitation across the area.",
